  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "e9siydnvE82uBlfe9RVyq97U1Oo=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:26:48.943Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The flag is short and operational: the Camunda partnership is not verified for these leads, and it should be confirmed before pitching. Four leads carry it \u2014 Consileon, Creetion and Senacor Technologies in DE and x1F in NL \u2014 all at \"3 \u00b7 later\".\n\nIt matters more than a typical data-quality caveat because the entire Camunda approach presupposes the relationship. The demo attaches to a Camunda job worker, the fit argument is about Camunda's mutable instance history, and the route into the account is the partner's existing Camunda practice. If the practice does not exist, none of that transfers; the lead would need re-researching from the tool column outward.\n\nThe flag is recoverable from the lead records themselves rather than only from this note: it is encoded twice, once in the source column (\"v5 \u00b7 Camunda cohort (unverified)\") and once in the tool column (\"Camunda (unverified)\"). These same four leads are also part of the ten-lead group that carries the sovereign-procurability Camunda demo, so exactly 40% of that demo's audience rests on an unconfirmed premise. Creetion additionally appears in the Flowable partner cohort, which may make it reachable on a verified footing regardless.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (4)\n\n**3 \u00b7 later**\n- Consileon (DE)\n- Creetion (NL)\n- Senacor Technologies (DE)\n- x1F (DE)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> CAMUNDA PARTNERSHIP NOT VERIFIED \u2014 confirm before pitching",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:26:48.943845Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:26:48.943849Z"
    }
  },
